CPU thermal zones: wire the throttling ladder upstream never added for SM8450.

The CPU zones carry passive trips (90 C and 95 C, 2 C hysteresis, critical at
110 C) and every CPU has #cooling-cells, but no zone has a cooling map: the
per-policy cpufreq cooling devices that cpufreq.c registers
(policy->cdev = of_cpufreq_cooling_register(policy)) are never called, so when
a sensor crosses its trip the OS has no way to reduce CPU frequency - only the
hardware LMh limiter reacts.  sm8250 (same QCom pattern, 24 cooling-device
entries) does wire it; this is the same shape, one cooling device per policy:

  cpu0..cpu3 zones   -> &cpu0   (policy0, the A510 quad)
  cpu4..cpu6 zones   -> &cpu4   (policy4, the A710 trio)
  cpu7 zones         -> &cpu7   (policy7, the X2)

polling-delay-passive 250 ms matches sm8250's CPU zones (GPU zones keep their
10 ms).  Trips are untouched: nothing throttles below 90 C.

Measured before this change on the unit (7.2.5, 38 zones): the only cooling
maps in the live device tree were the two GPU ones, and cpufreq-cpu0/4/7 were
bound to no trip.

The map must name the policy's first CPU: that node is the of_node of the
cooling device cpufreq.c registered, so &cpu1..&cpu3 (which sm8250 also lists)
would resolve to nothing.

